# t27ai-bot env.example -- the bot's half (root Dockerfile image, the Telegraf
# monolith). Copy to /opt/t27ai-render/bot.env, chmod 600. NAMES only: the
# owner writes the values on the VPS himself; nothing here is a secret.
#
# The bot token of @t27ai_bot, from @BotFather. The slot name is the
# monolith's own: BOT_TOKEN_12 is t27ai_bot (src/bot.ts). Only the slots
# present here are launched, so this one file starts exactly one bot.
BOT_TOKEN_12=
# RENDER_API_KEY (the bot's server key for POST /api/course/key,
# src/services/courseAccess.ts) is NOT written here: the unit reads it from
# /opt/t27ai-bot/render-key.env, copied on the VPS from the ladder's .env.
# Writing it here too is harmless: render-key.env is read last and wins.
# Where the render answers from the bot's side: the in-network name of the
# LADDER unit (it serves /api/course/*; the render on 8090 does not). The
# service unit sets the same value after the env files, so it always wins.
RENDER_BASE_URL=http://t27ai-ladder:8080
NODE_ENV=production
INFISICAL_ENVIRONMENT=prod
# The monolith's data store and the rest of its secrets: today they come from
# Infisical (INFISICAL_CLIENT_ID, INFISICAL_CLIENT_SECRET, INFISICAL_PROJECT_ID)
# or are set here directly (SUPABASE_URL, SUPABASE_SERVICE_KEY, ...). The
# Railway gateway they pointed at is gone: that is an owner decision, see
# README, "Needs the owner".
